# NextKey — revocation, and its honest limit.
# ENSv2 hackathon deployment on Sepolia. Recorded 2026-09-06.
#
# The README claims that access can be withdrawn and that who may withdraw it
# is enforced by the setter role on the name. This is that claim, executed.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Before: Anna can open the secret.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> node scripts/nextkey.mjs open visa anna

  visa.nextkey.eth  opened as anna
  witch collapse practice feed shame open despair creek road again ice least

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. The owner withdraws it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs revoke visa anna.nextkey.eth

  revoking anna.nextkey.eth's access to visa.nextkey.eth
  grant record  nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf
  the ciphertext stays; the wrapped key is cleared
  → 0xa89511166f9dd3c2a0f2918c7063b273da9e28458bb1578a428927903e67905b success

  Note what this does and does not do. Anyone who already decrypted
  the secret still knows it — no system can retract knowledge. What ends is
  future access, and who may end it is enforced by the setter role on the name.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. After: Anna cannot.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> node scripts/nextkey.mjs open visa anna

  Error: no grant at nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf for "anna"
         — access was never given, or was revoked

# The ciphertext at visa.nextkey.eth is untouched and still public. What ended
# is Anna's ability to unwrap the key to it. Nothing was deleted from her
# machine and nothing needed to be: the wrapped key lived on chain, in a record
# only an account holding the setter role could clear.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. Restored, so the live demo keeps working.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs share visa alice anna.nextkey.eth

  sharing visa.nextkey.eth with anna.nextkey.eth
  their public key comes from their own ENS record — they never registered with us
  grant record  nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf
  → 0xe83544fbcb128fecd64fec8839fe37a96a0460b57d7227bd604c488e3ca4826f success

# The grant returns to the same address, because a grant is addressed by the
# recipient's key and Anna's key did not change. That is the property the
# fingerprint scheme was chosen for.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What this does not demonstrate
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# That Anna forgot the secret. She did not, and no product can arrange that.
# Revocation ends future access; it cannot retract knowledge already obtained.
# A system claiming otherwise would be lying, and the note printed by `revoke`
# says so at the moment a user is most likely to assume the opposite.
#
# Nor does it stop a third party who copied the grant record while it was
# public from decrypting it — if they hold Anna's private key. They do not,
# which is the whole point of wrapping to a key rather than storing a
# permission. But "revoked" means "no longer granted", not "unreadable to
# anyone who ever held the key".
